Because .results-header now wraps both the <h1> and the <p>.

So this:

.results-header {
  display: block;
  width: fit-content;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding-bottom: 8px;
  border-bottom: 1.5px solid #d6ddf0;
}

applies to the whole wrapper, not just the title.

That’s why the divider stretches under the paragraph too.

If you want only “Your Matches” centered with a short line underneath, style the h1 instead:

.results-header {
  text-align: center;
}

.results-header h1 {
  display: inline-block;
  padding-bottom: 8px;
  border-bottom: 1.5px solid #d6ddf0;
}

Then your paragraph can stay below it.

If you also want the paragraph centered:

.results-header p {
  text-align: center;
}

So the distinction is:

.results-header     = whole section wrapper
.results-header h1  = just the title

Right now you’re centering/styling the wrapper, which contains both elements.

in small scfeens

That’s happening because your paragraph container is becoming way too narrow on the small screen.

The text isn’t broken — it’s just wrapping after almost every word.

Likely cause

You probably have something like:

.results-header {
  width: fit-content;
}

Since .results-header contains both the <h1> and the <p>, fit-content can make that whole wrapper shrink strangely on narrow screens.

For mobile, remove that behavior and let the wrapper use the available width:

@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .results-header {
    width: 100%;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .results-header p {
    width: auto;
    padding: 0 20px;
  }
}

And keep the short underline only on the title:

.results-header h1 {
  display: inline-block;
  padding-bottom: 8px;
  border-bottom: 1.5px solid #d6ddf0;
}

The key change is:

.results-header {
  width: 100%;
}

instead of width: fit-content;.

Right now the wrapper is shrinking to a narrow column, which is why the paragraph looks like a vertical word list.

